questions.modelAnswer:
(a)
def merge_sort(a):
    if len(a) <= 1:
        return a
    mid = len(a) // 2
    left = merge_sort(a[:mid])
    right = merge_sort(a[mid:])
    return merge(left, right)

def merge(left, right):
    out, i, j = [], 0, 0
    while i < len(left) and j < len(right):
        if left[i] <= right[j]:
            out.append(left[i]); i += 1
        else:
            out.append(right[j]); j += 1
    out.extend(left[i:])
    out.extend(right[j:])
    return out

(To sort trades by price, compare t.price, or pass a key function.)

(b) Split into [38, 27, 43] and [3, 9, 82, 10]. The left half splits into [38] and [27, 43], giving [27, 38, 43]. The right half splits into [3, 9] and [82, 10], giving [3, 9] and [10, 82], which merge to [3, 9, 10, 82]. The final merge of [27, 38, 43] and [3, 9, 10, 82] gives [3, 9, 10, 27, 38, 43, 82].

(c) Sorting n items means two sorts of n/2 items plus a merge that is linear in n: T(n) = 2T(n/2) + cn, with T(1) = c. At depth k there are 2ᵏ subproblems of size n/2ᵏ, costing cn in total. The recursion stops at depth log₂ n, so T(n) = cn log₂ n + cn = Θ(n log n). This holds for every input, because the split is always balanced.

(d) Keeping equal prices in their original (time) order requires a stable sort. Merge sort is stable when the merge takes from the left half on ties (the <= test), because elements from the left half came earlier in the input. Standard in-place quicksort partitions with long-distance swaps, so it is not stable. Python's built-in sorted(trades, key=lambda t: t.price) is stable and would also work.

(e) Quicksort is quadratic when the pivot is repeatedly the smallest or largest element, so each partition removes only one element. With a first-element pivot this happens on sorted or reverse-sorted input, giving n(n − 1)/2 comparisons, for example 499,500 for 1,000 trades. In practice, a random pivot or median-of-three makes the expected time O(n log n) on any input, and library sorts use hybrids (introsort switches to heapsort, and Python uses Timsort).
